Field extraction
awk '{print $1}' file.txt

Print only the first column of every line
awk '{print $1, $3, $NF}' file.txt

Print columns 1, 3, and last column
awk -F: '{print $1}' /etc/passwd

Use : as delimiter — print usernames from passwd file
awk -F, '{print $2, $4}' data.csv

Process CSV file — print 2nd and 4th columns
awk 'BEGIN{OFS=","} {print $1,$2,$3}' file

Print columns separated by comma — convert to CSV
awk '{print NR, $0}' file.txt

Add line numbers to every line — like cat -n
Filtering rows
awk '/error/ {print $0}' app.log

Print only lines containing "error" — like grep but more powerful
awk '$3 > 1000 {print $1, $3}' file.txt

Print name and value only where column 3 is greater than 1000
awk 'NR==5 {print}' file.txt

Print only line 5 — like sed -n '5p'
awk 'NR>=10 && NR<=20 {print}' file.txt

Print lines 10 to 20 only
awk '$2 ~ /^admin/ {print $1,$2}' users.txt

Match regex on field 2 only — print users whose role starts with admin
awk '$1 !~ /^#/ {print}' config.conf

Skip comment lines — print only non-comment lines from config
Calculations & math
awk '{sum+=$3} END{print "Total:", sum}' file

Sum all values in column 3 — total sales, bytes, counts
awk '{sum+=$3} END{print "Avg:", sum/NR}' file

Calculate average of column 3 across all lines
awk 'BEGIN{max=0} $3>max{max=$3} END{print "Max:", max}' file
Find maximum value in column 3

awk '/error/{count++} END{print count}' app.log
Count total error lines — like grep -c but with more control
